# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Attacker can extract more tokens/funds than intended each time the settlement actions are rerun

## Proof of Concept
**There is no check that the settlement is actually in a "failed" state before retrying in the retrySettlement function. This could allow an attacker to cause it to fallback multiple times when it should only fallback once.**
**The affected code is in the lzReceive function:**


    function lzReceive(uint16 _srcChainId, bytes calldata _srcAddress, uint64, bytes calldata _payload) public {
    // ...

    } else if (_payload[0] == 0x09) {

    // Parse nonce
    nonce = uint32(bytes4(_payload[PARAMS_START:PARAMS_TKN_START]));

    // Reopen Settlement for redemption
    getSettlement[nonce].status = STATUS_FAILED;

    // Emit LogFallback
    emit LogFallback(nonce, _srcChainId);

    }

    // ...
    }


**When a payload with flag 0x09 is received, it simply sets the settlement status to STATUS_FAILED without checking if it was already in a failed state.**

**An attacker could exploit this by:**

**- Initiate a settlement**
**- Cause it to fail via revert/out-of-gas**
**- Keep sending payloads with 0x09 flag to trigger multiple fallback calls**

**This would cause the fallback function to be called multiple times for the same failed settlement, potentially draining funds.**
## Tools Used
Manual
## Recommended Mitigation Steps

**The contract should check the current settlement status before setting to failed:**


    if (getSettlement[nonce].status != STATUS_FAILED) {
    getSettlement[nonce].status = STATUS_FAILED;

    // Emit LogFallback
    emit LogFallback(nonce, _srcChainId); 
    }
**This ensures the fallback is only triggered once per failed settlement.**


