# Vulnerability details

## Summary
A malicious user can deliberately set an irrelevant (or even poisonous) globaltoken, as anyone can directly access addGlobalToken on the `CoreBranchRouter`

This issue is extremely similar to the issue found in the last Maia DAO contest where a user could add a local token due to lack of access control. That issue can be seen [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-05-maia-findings/issues/285). The core root of the issue is that `addGlobalToken` lacks an access control modifier. When the function is called, it encodes the function parameters and then calls the `callOut` function on the bridge agent. This is where the issue exists. `callOut` does not have the `requiresRouter` modifier that allows one the Router to call it. In this case, it will continue to execute and allow a random user to add a global token or as many as they like.

The following POC shows you a call made to `addGlobalToken` by a random address. The test will pass if ran like this, even though we are pranking the random address and calling `encodeCallNoDeposit()`.

## Impact
There are a few different impacts that this could have.

1. If a malicious contract is set, gas griefing is possible.
2. If a regular ERC20 contract with admin minting permissions is set, the exploiter can mint an unlimited amount of local tokens for himself. By bridging them, he can receive an arbitrary amount of global tokens at the root chain.

