# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The vulnerability arises from the fact that the managePortStrategy function performs a cross-chain call to another contract (IBridgeAgent), and if the _dstChainId is incorrect or points to an unsupported chain, the refund mechanism may not work as expected. This can result in funds becoming stuck on the destination chain, making them burnt on source chain and inaccessible on destination chain.

## Proof of Concept


    function managePortStrategy(
        address _portStrategy,
        address _underlyingToken,
        uint256 _dailyManagementLimit,
        bool _isUpdateDailyLimit,
        address _refundee,
        uint16 _dstChainId,
        GasParams calldata _gParams
    ) external payable onlyOwner {
        // Encode CallData
        bytes memory params = abi.encode(_portStrategy, _underlyingToken, _dailyManagementLimit, _isUpdateDailyLimit);

        // Pack funcId into data
        bytes memory payload = abi.encodePacked(bytes1(0x06), params);

        //Add new global token to branch chain
        IBridgeAgent(bridgeAgentAddress).callOut{value: msg.value}(
            payable(_refundee), _refundee, _dstChainId, payload, _gParams
        );
    }

The function performs the cross-chain call using the provided _dstChainId.

If the _dstChainId is not correctly configured on the other chain (or does not exist), the refund mechanism might not work as intended.

As a result, funds sent as part of the cross-chain call may become stuck on the source chain, and the user might not be able to retrieve them.



